B = B - A = (-1, 1, 0), AD = D - A = (-1/2, 0, 1/2)\n\nNormal = AB \u00d7 AD = |i  j  k; -1  1  0; -1/2  0  1/2|\n= i(1\u00b71/2 - 0\u00b70) - j((-1)(1/2) - 0\u00b7(-1/2)) + k((-1)(0) - 1\u00b7(-1/2))\n= i(1/2) - j(-1/2) + k(1/2)\n= (1/2, 1/2, 1/2)\n\nSo the normal is proportional to (1,1,1). The plane equation: x + y + z = 1.\n\nCheck: A(1,0,0): 1+0+0 = 1 \u2713\nB(0,1,0): 0+1+0 = 1 \u2713\nD(1/2, 0, 1/2): 1/2 + 0 + 1/2 = 1 \u2713\n\nSo the triangle lies in the plane x + y + z = 1.\n\nNow, for the volume by rotation about the x-axis, I can use the method of washers/disks.\n\nAt a fixed x value, the cross-section of the triangle in the yz-plane is a line segment. When this line segment is rotated about the origin (in the yz-plane), it sweeps out an annular region (a washer).\n\nLet me parameterize the cross-section at a given x.\n\nFor x \u2208 [0, 1/2]:\nThe triangle at this x is bounded by:\n- Edge AB: (y, z) = (1-x, 0)\n- Edge BD: (y, z) = (1-2x, x)\n- Edge DA doesn't exist at x < 1/2\n\nWait, I need to think about this more carefully. The triangle has vertices at x = 1 (point A), x = 0 (point B), and x = 1/2 (point D).\n\nFor x \u2208 [0, 1/2]:\n- The edges that pass through this x-value are AB and BD.\n- AB at x: y = 1-x, z = 0\n- BD at x: y = 1-2x, z = x\n\nSo the cross-section is the line segment from (1-2x, x) to (1-x, 0) in the yz-plane.\n\nFor x \u2208 [1/2, 1]:\n- The edges that pass through this x-value are AB and DA.\n- AB at x: y = 1-x, z = 0\n- DA at x: y = 0, z = 1-x\n\nSo the cross-section is the line segment from (0, 1-x) to (1-x, 0) in the yz-plane.\n\nWhen we rotate a line segment about the origin in the yz-plane, the resulting region is an annulus (washer) with inner radius equal to the minimum distance from the origin to the line segment, and outer radius equal to the maximum distance from the origin to the line segment.\n\nWait, actually, I need to be more careful. When we rotate a line segment about a point (the origin), the resulting region 