\u2208 [1/3, 1/2].\n\nSo:\n- For x \u2208 [0, 1/3]: the minimum distance is at s = 0, i.e., at point P1 = (1-2x, x). The minimum distance is \u221a(1-4x+5x\u00b2).\n  The maximum distance: we need to check endpoints. d\u00b2(0) = 1-4x+5x\u00b2 and d\u00b2(1) = (1-x)\u00b2 + 0 = (1-x)\u00b2.\n  Compare: 1-4x+5x\u00b2 vs (1-x)\u00b2 = 1-2x+x\u00b2\n  1-4x+5x\u00b2 - (1-2x+x\u00b2) = -2x+4x\u00b2 = 2x(2x-1)\n  For x \u2208 [0, 1/3]: 2x-1 < 0, so 1-4x+5x\u00b2 < (1-x)\u00b2.\n  So the maximum is at s = 1: distance = 1-x.\n\n- For x \u2208 [1/3, 1/2]: the minimum is at s* = (3x-1)/(2x).\n  d\u00b2(s*) = (1-4x+5x\u00b2) - (x-3x\u00b2)\u00b2/(2x\u00b2)\n  Let me compute (x-3x\u00b2)\u00b2/(2x\u00b2) = x\u00b2(1-3x)\u00b2/(2x\u00b2) = (1-3x)\u00b2/2\n\n  So d\u00b2(s*) = 1-4x+5x\u00b2 - (1-3x)\u00b2/2\n  = 1-4x+5x\u00b2 - (1-6x+9x\u00b2)/2\n  = (2-8x+10x\u00b2 - 1+6x-9x\u00b2)/2\n  = (1-2x+x\u00b2)/2\n  = (1-x)\u00b2/2\n\n  The maximum is at one of the endpoints. d\u00b2(0) = 1-4x+5x\u00b2 and d\u00b2(1) = (1-x)\u00b2.\n  As computed above, for x \u2208 [1/3, 1/2], 2x(2x-1) \u2265 0 (since x \u2265 1/3 > 0 and 2x-1 < 0 for x < 1/2), so 1-4x+5x\u00b2 < (1-x)\u00b2.\n  So the maximum is at s = 1: distance = 1-x.\n\nSo for x \u2208 [0, 1/2]:\n- Inner radius: r(x) = \u221a(1-4x+5x\u00b2) for x \u2208 [0, 1/3], and r(x) = (1-x)/\u221a2 for x \u2208 [1/3, 1/2]\n- Outer radius: R(x) = 1-x for all x \u2208 [0, 1/2]\n\nWait, but I need to double-check this. The minimum distance from the origin to the line segment gives the inner radius, and the maximum gives the outer radius. But I should verify that the line segment doesn't \"wrap around\" the origin in a way that creates a non-annular shape.\n\nActually, since we're rotating a line segment about a point, and the line segment doesn't pass through the point (the origin), the result is indeed an annular region. The inner radius is the minimum distance from the origin to any point on the segment, and the outer radius is the maximum distance.\n\nHmm wait, but that's not quite right either. When you rotate a line segment about a point, the swept region is not necessarily an annulus. Consider a line segment that passes \"behind\" the origin - the rotation would create a full disk.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. When we rotate a set S in the yz-plane about the origin, the result is the set of all points whose distance from the origin equals the distance from the origin to some point in S. So the result is a union of circles centered at the origin, with radii equal to the distances from the origin to points in S.\n\nIf S is a line segment, the set of distances from the origin to points on the segment is an interval [r_min, r_max] (since the distance function is continuous on a connected set). So the result is indeed an annulus with inner radius r_min and outer radius r_max.\n\nWait, but that's only if the distance function takes all values between r_min and r_max. Since the line segment is connected and the distance function is continuous, by the intermediate value theorem, yes, it takes all values between r_min and r_max.\n\nSo the area of the cross-section at position x is \u03c0(R\u00b2 - r\u00b2).\n\nLet me now set up the integral.\n\nV = \u222b\u2080\u00b9 A(x) dx where A(x) is the area of the cross-section.\n\nFor x \u2208 [0, 1/3]:\nA(x) = \u03c0((1-x)\u00b2 - (1-4x+5x\u00b2))\n= \u03c0((1 - 2x + x\u00b2) - (1 - 4x + 5x\u00b2))\n= \u03c0(2x - 4x\u00b2)\n= 2\u03c0x(1 - 2x)\n\nFor x \u2208 [1/3, 1/2]:\nA(x) = \u03c0((1-x)\u00b2 - (1-x)\u00b2/2)\n= \u03c0(1-x)\u00b2/2\n\nFor x \u2208 [1/2, 1]:\nA(x) = \u03c0((1-x)\u00b2 - (1-x)\u00b2/2)\n= \u03c0(1-x)\u00b2/2\n\nSo V = \u222b\u2080^{1/3} 2\u03c0x(1-2x) dx + \u222b_{1/3}^{1/2} \u03c0(1-x)\u00b2/2 dx + \u222b_{1/2}^{1} \u03c0(1-x)\u00b2/2 dx\n\nLet me compute each integral.\n\nI\u2081 = \u222b\u2080^{1/3} 2\u03c0x(1-2x) dx = 2\u03c0 \u222b\u2080^{1/3} (x - 2x\u00b2) dx\n= 2\u03c0 [x\u00b2/2 - 2x\u00b3/3]\u2080^{1/3}\n= 2\u03c0 [(1/9)/2 - 2(1/27)/3]\n= 2\u03c0 [1/18 - 2/81]\n= 2\u03c0 [9/162 - 4/162]\n= 2\u03c0 \u00b7 5/162\n= 10\u03c0/162\n= 5\u03c0/81\n\nI\u2082 = \u222b_{1/3}^{1/2} \u03c0(1-x)\u00b2/2 dx\n\nLet u = 1-x, du = -dx. When x = 1/3, u = 2/3. When x = 1/2, u = 1/2.\nI\u2082 = \u03c0/2 \u222b_{1/2}^{2/3} u\u00b2 du = \u03c0/2 [u\u00b3/3]_{1/2}^{2/3}\n= \u03c0/2 \u00b7 [1/3][(2/3)\u00b3 - (1/2)\u00b3]\n= \u03c0/6 \u00b7 [8/27 - 1/8]\n= \u03c0/6 \u00b7 [64/216 - 27/216]\n= \u03c0/6 \u00b7 37/216\n= 37\u03c0/1296\n\nI\u2083 = \u222b_{1/2}^{1} \u03c0(1-x)\u00b2/2 dx\n\nLet u = 1-x, du = -dx. When x = 1/2, u = 1/2. When x = 1, u = 0.\nI\u2083 = \u03c0/2 \u222b\u2080^{1/2} u\u00b2 du = \u03c0/2 \u00b7 [u\u00b3/3]\u2080^{1/2}\n= \u03c0/2 \u00b7 1/3 \u00b7 1/8\n= \u03c0/48\n\nSo V = 5\u03c0/81 + 37\u03c0/1296 + \u03c0/48\n\nLet me find a common denominator. The LCM of 81, 1296, 48.\n81 = 3\u2074\n1296 = 6\u2074 = 2\u2074 \u00b7 3\u2074\n48 = 2\u2074 \u00b7 3\n\nLCM = 2\u2074 \u00b7 3\u2074 = 1296\n\n5\u03c0/81 = 5\u03c0 \u00b7 16/1296 = 80\u03c0/1296\n37\u03c0/1296 = 37\u03c0/1296\n\u03c0/48 = \u03c0 \u00b7 27/1296 = 27\u03c0/1296\n\nV = (80 + 37 + 27)\u03c0/1296 = 144\u03c0/1296 = \u03c0/9\n\nSo the volume is \u03c0/9.\n\nWait, let me double-check my computation of I\u2081.\n\nI\u2081 = 2\u03c0 \u222b\u2080^{1/3} (x - 2x\u00b2) dx\n= 2\u03c0 [x\u00b2/2 - 2x\u00b3/3]\u2080^{1/3}\n= 2\u03c0 [(1/3)\u00b2/2 - 2(1/3)\u00b3/3]\n= 2\u03c0 [1/18 - 2/81]\n= 2\u03c0 [9/162 - 4/162]\n= 2\u03c0 \u00b7 5/162\n= 10\u03c0/162\n= 5\u03c0/81 \u2713\n\nLet me double-check I\u2082.\nI\u2082 = \u03c0/2 \u222b_{1/2}^{2/3} u\u00b2 du = \u03c0/2 \u00b7 [u\u00b3/3]_{1/2}^{2/3}\n= \u03c0/2 \u00b7 1/3 \u00b7 [(2/3)\u00b3 - (1/2)\u00b3]\n= \u03c0/6 \u00b7 [8/27 - 1/8]\n= \u03c0/6 \u00b7 [(64-27)/216]\n= \u03c0/6 \u00b7 37/216\n= 37\u03c0/1296 \u2713\n\nI\u2083 = \u03c0/2 \u00b7 [u\u00b3/3]\u2080^{1/2} = \u03c0/2 \u00b7 1/3 \u00b7 (1/8) = \u03c0/48 \u2713\n\nV = 80\u03c0/1296 + 37\u03c0/1296 + 27\u03c0/1296 = 144\u03c0/1296\n\n144/1296 = 1/9\n\nSo V = \u03c0/9.\n\nHmm, let me verify this with a different approach to make sure.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the problem. When we rotate the triangle about the x-axis, I should think about what solid is formed.\n\nThe triangle ABD lies in the plane x + y + z = 1. Let me think about what happens when we rotate this about the x-axis.\n\nActually, let me use Pappus's theorem or the shell method as a check.\n\nAlternatively, let me verify by directly thinking about the geometry.\n\nThe triangle has vertices A(1,0,0), B(0,1,0), D(1/2,0,1/2).\n\nWhen rotated about the x-axis:\n- A is on the x-axis, so it stays fixed.\n- B(0,1,0) rotates to form a circle of radius 1 in the yz-plane at x=0.\n- D(1/2,0,1/2) rotates to form a circle of radius 1/2 in the yz-plane at x=1/2.\n\nThe solid is bounded by the surfaces of revolution of the three edges.\n\nEdge AB: from (1,0,0) to (0,1,0). This is the line y = 1-x, z = 0. When rotated about the x-axis, this generates a cone (actually, a surface of revolution). The distance from the x-axis to this line at position x is |y| = 1-x (since z=0). So this generates a cone with the tip at x=1 and base radius 1 at x=0.\n\nEdge DA: from (1/2,0,1/2) to (1,0,0). This is the line z = 1-x, y = 0 for x \u2208 [1/2, 1]. When rotated about the x-axis, the distance from the x-axis is |z| = 1-x. So this also generates a cone with tip at x=1 and base radius 1/2 at x=1/2.\n\nEdge BD: from (0,1,0) to (1/2,0,1/2). Parametrize: (t/2, 1-t, t/2) for t \u2208 [0,1]. The distance from the x-axis is \u221a((1-t)\u00b2 + (t/2)\u00b2). At x = t/2, t = 2x, so distance = \u221a((1-2x)\u00b2 + x\u00b2) = \u221a(1-4x+5x\u00b2).\n\nSo the solid is bounded by:\n- For x \u2208 [0, 1/2]: the outer surface is from edge AB (radius 1-x) and the inner surface is from edge BD (radius \u221a(1-4x+5x\u00b2)).\n- For x \u2208 [1/2, 1]: the outer surface is from edge AB (radius 1-x) and the inner surface is from edge DA (radius 1-x... wait, that can't be right).\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. For x \u2208 [1/2, 1], the cross-section of the triangle is the line segment from (0, 1-x) to (1-x, 0) in the yz-plane. The distance from the origin to (0, 1-x) is 1-x, and the distance from the origin to (1-x, 0) is also 1-x. The minimum distance from the origin to this line segment is (1-x)/\u221a2 (at the point where the perpendicular from the origin meets the line y+z = 1-x).\n\nSo the outer radius is 1-x and the inner radius is (1-x)/\u221a2.\n\nBut wait, what surface does this correspond to? The edge AB gives the point (1-x, 0) at distance 1-x. The edge DA gives the point (0, 1-x) at distance 1-x. Both give the same outer radius. The inner radius (1-x)/\u221a2 corresponds to the closest point on the line segment to the origin, which is the midpoint of the segment: ((1-x)/2, (1-x)/2).\n\nSo for x \u2208 [1/2, 1], the cross-section is a washer with outer radius 1-x and inner radius (1-x)/\u221a2.\n\nThis is consistent with what I had before.\n\nLet me also double-check the case x \u2208 [0, 1/3]. At x = 0:\n- P1 = (1, 0) [from edge BD: y = 1-0 = 1, z = 0]. Distance = 1.\n- P2 = (1, 0) [from edge AB: y = 1-0 = 1, z = 0]. Distance = 1.\n\nWait, that's the same point! At x = 0, both edges AB and BD meet at B(0,1,0). So the cross-section is just a single point at distance 1 from the origin. The washer has zero area.\n\nLet me check: A(0) = 2\u03c0(0)(1-0) = 0. \u2713\n\nAt x = 1/3:\n- P1 = (1-2/3, 1/3) = (1/3, 1/3). Distance = \u221a(1/9 + 1/9) = \u221a(2/9) = \u221a2/3.\n- P2 = (1-1/3, 0) = (2/3, 0). Distance = 2/3.\n\nA(1/3) = 2\u03c0(1/3)(1-2/3) = 2\u03c0(1/3)(1/3) = 2\u03c0/9.\n\nUsing the other formula: \u03c0(1-x)\u00b2/2 = \u03c0(2/3)\u00b2/2 = \u03c0(4/9)/2 = 2\u03c0/9. \u2713\n\nGreat, the two formulas agree at x = 1/3, confirming the transition point.\n\nSo the volume is V = \u03c0/9.\n\nActually, I want to double-check this answer using a completely different method. Let me use the theorem of Pappus or direct integration in a different coordinate system.\n\nActually, let me try to use the formula for the volume of a solid of revolution using the \"shell\" method or by considering the triangle as a region and applying the appropriate formula.\n\nThe volume of the solid obtained by rotating a planar region about an axis in the same plane can be computed using:\nV = 2\u03c0 \u222c_R d(x,y,z) dA\n\nwhere d(x,y,z) is the distance from the point to the axis of rotation, and the integral is over the region R.\n\nWait, that's not quite right. The correct formula (from Pappus's centroid theorem generalized) is:\n\nV = \u222b\u222b_R 2\u03c0 \u00b7 r(x,y,z) dA\n\nwhere r is the distance to the axis and dA is the area element in the plane containing R.\n\nHmm, but this formula gives the volume of the solid of revolution when the region doesn't cross the axis. Let me think about whether this applies here.\n\nActually, the formula V = \u222b\u222b_R 2\u03c0r dA is correct when R is on one side of the axis and doesn't cross it. The idea is that each infinitesimal area element dA at distance r from the axis sweeps out a volume 2\u03c0r \u00b7 dA when rotated.\n\nBut wait, this formula works when the region is in a plane that contains the axis. In our case, the triangle is in the plane x + y + z = 1, which does NOT contain the x-axis (the x-axis is y = z = 0, and substituting gives x = 1, which is just a point, not the whole axis).\n\nSo Pappus's theorem in its standard form doesn't directly apply.\n\nLet me try yet another approach. Let me use the \"washer\" method more carefully.\n\nActually, I already did the washer method and got V = \u03c0/9. Let me just verify the arithmetic one more time.\n\nV = 5\u03c0/81 + 37\u03c0/1296 + \u03c0/48\n\nCommon denominator = 1296.\n\n5\u03c0/81 = 5\u03c0 \u00d7 16/1296 = 80\u03c0/1296\n37\u03c0/1296 = 37\u03c0/1296\n\u03c0/48 = \u03c0 \u00d7 27/1296 = 27\u03c0/1296\n\nSum = (80 + 37 + 27)\u03c0/1296 = 144\u03c0/1296\n\n144/1296: divide both by 144: 1/9.\n\nSo V = \u03c0/9.\n\nI'm confident the answer is \u03c0/9.\n"
